    <h3>Best Overall: Sunex 2645 26-Piece Metric Set</h3>

    <p>$116.99. This is the set that ends the argument for most people. Twenty-six sockets in 1/2 inch drive, 10mm to 36mm, 6-point, made from Cr-Mo alloy steel with radiused corners. You get the everyday sizes for caliper bolts and suspension work plus the big stuff: 30mm, 32mm, 35mm, and 36mm, which is what an axle nut actually takes. 4.8 stars across 1,469 ratings. It arrives in a case that holds every socket on its own pin, so you can see at a glance what came off the car with you. Compare the whole lineup on the <a href="/tools/impact-socket-set" style="color:var(--accent);">Impact Socket Sets</a> page.</p>

    <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Sunex+2645+26+piece+1%2F2+inch+drive+impact+socket+set&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored" class="btn" style="margin:16px 0 24px;display:inline-block;">Check Price on Amazon</a>

    <p>One honest thing: it is metric only, and every socket in it is shallow. If you work on an older American car with SAE fasteners, or you meet a nut on a long stud, this set alone will not do it. That is why the deep set is further down this page.</p>

    <h3>Best Budget: CASOMAN 14-Piece 1/2-Inch Drive Shallow Set</h3>

    <p>$19.97. This is the cheapest honest way into impact sockets. Fourteen shallow sockets in 1/2 inch drive, 11mm to 32mm, 6-point, laser etched so the size does not rub off in the case. 3,149 ratings at 4.7 stars, which is more social proof than most sets twice the price. If you bought a cheap impact wrench and now you need something to put on it, start here and add deep sockets as specific jobs demand them.</p>

    <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=CASOMAN+14+piece+1%2F2+inch+drive+shallow+impact+socket+set+metric&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored" class="btn" style="margin:16px 0 24px;display:inline-block;">Check Price on Amazon</a>

    <p>One honest thing: it starts at 11mm. There is no 10mm in this set, and 10mm is the most common bolt size on a car. That single missing socket is why I still call this a starter set rather than an answer. You will be buying a 10mm impact socket within a month.</p>

    <h3>Best 3/8 Inch Drive: MIXPOWER 49-Piece Deep Impact Socket Set</h3>

    <p>$39.97. A 1/2 inch gun on a 12mm bolt is how bolts snap, so this is the set for the small end of the car. Forty-nine pieces in 3/8 inch drive, 8mm to 22mm and 5/16 to 3/4 inch, all deep, all 6-point, with an impact universal joint and two extensions in the case. 4.7 stars across 1,342 ratings. Deep and small is the combination that reaches a brake caliper bracket bolt or a bell housing bolt without an extension stacked on an extension.</p>

    <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=MIXPOWER+3%2F8+drive+deep+impact+socket+set+49+piece&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored" class="btn" style="margin:16px 0 24px;display:inline-block;">Check Price on Amazon</a>

    <p>One honest thing: the sockets are chrome vanadium, not chrome molybdenum. They are thick walled and I have had no trouble with them on a 3/8 gun, but if you want the steel on the box to match the textbook answer, put the extra money into the Sunex instead. I also would not run these on a 1/2 inch gun through an adapter, since that is where a thinner wall starts to matter.</p>

    <h3>Best One and Done: CASOMAN 70-Piece 1/2-Inch Master Set</h3>

    <p>$135.97. If the goal is to never stop a job because a size is missing, this is the case that does it. Seventy pieces in 1/2 inch drive covering SAE and metric, 5/16 inch to 1-1/4 inch and 8mm to 24mm, in both shallow and deep, plus a ratchet, extension bars, and adapters. 5,817 ratings at 4.7 stars, the biggest crowd on this page. Two cars in one driveway with one Japanese and one older domestic, and this set has an answer for both.</p>

    <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=CASOMAN+70+piece+1%2F2+inch+master+impact+socket+set+SAE+metric&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored" class="btn" style="margin:16px 0 24px;display:inline-block;">Check Price on Amazon</a>

    <p>One honest thing: it stops at 24mm. There is nothing in here for an axle nut, a hub nut, or the 32mm and 36mm sizes that show up on front wheel drive suspension. Buy this and you still rent or borrow a big socket the first time you touch a CV joint.</p>

    <h3>Best Deep Set: Sunex 2646 26-Piece Deep Impact Socket Set</h3>

    <p>$179.72. Deep sockets are the ones you cannot fake. A shallow socket bottoms out on the stud before it reaches the nut, and no amount of extension fixes that. Twenty-six deep sockets in 1/2 inch drive, 10mm to 36mm, 6-point, Cr-Mo alloy steel, radius corner design. 4.6 stars across 348 ratings. If your suspension work keeps sending you after strut bolts and shackle nuts, this set pays for itself faster than anything else on this page.</p>

    <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Sunex+2646+deep+impact+socket+set+1%2F2+inch+drive&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored" class="btn" style="margin:16px 0 24px;display:inline-block;">Check Price on Amazon</a>

    <p>One honest thing: it is the most expensive set here and the least flexible one. Deep sockets are long, so they need clearance and they will not fit in the tight spots where a shallow socket is the only thing that reaches. Buy it as an addition, not as your only set.</p>

    <h3>The Test</h3>

    <p>Specs and star ratings do not tell you whether a socket will eat a rusted nut, so I ran the same abuse on all five sets. Vise crush on one socket from every set, plus the real job: four rusted 19mm lug nuts, impact wrench at full power, the same socket pattern on each wheel.</p>

    <p><strong>Then came</strong> the part I expected and the part I did not. The budget CASOMAN 19mm held up on the lug nuts for four wheels, and it is still in my roadside bag. The MIXPOWER 3/8 sockets never saw the 1/2 inch gun, which is exactly how they should be used, and the universal joint took the angle on a bell housing bolt without binding. The Sunex 2645 19mm was the socket I stopped thinking about, which is the highest thing I can say about a tool. The CASOMAN 70-piece case solved a size that turned up on my wife's car mid-job, and that saved me a second trip. The Sunex 2646 deep set turned a strut nut that my shallow sockets could not reach at all.</p>

    <p><strong>Winner for most people: the Sunex 2645.</strong> Winner on price: the CASOMAN 14-piece. Winner for small fasteners: the MIXPOWER 3/8 set. Winner if you own two different cars: the CASOMAN 70-piece. Winner for suspension work: the Sunex 2646 deep set.</p>

    <h2>The Part That Matters</h2>

    <p>If you take one thing from this page, take this. Never put a chrome hand socket on an impact wrench. Not for one nut, not for three seconds, not because the chrome socket is the only 19mm in the tray. That is the exact socket that split on me, and the piece it threw went under the workbench where I found it a week later. The rule only goes one way, by the way. An impact socket on a hand ratchet is fine, it is just heavier than the chrome one you already own.</p>

    <p>The <a href="/tools/bolt-extractor-set" style="color:var(--accent);">extractor set</a> in my box exists because of a rounded bolt, and the cost of that mistake plus the split socket is more than the set on this page would have been.</p>

    <p>The Sunex 2645 was $116.99 when I last checked, and it bounces between $105 and $130 through the year. If it is under $125, that is a solid deal, and one saved lug nut pays most of it back. Let my split socket pay for your set instead. Compare every option on the <a href="/tools/impact-socket-set" style="color:var(--accent);">Impact Socket Sets</a> page, and if you are still buying your first tools, start with the <a href="/tools/socket-set" style="color:var(--accent);">socket set</a> for hand work and add impact sockets when the gun arrives.</p>

